<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Neural Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Neural Plasticity</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2090-5904</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1687-5443</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">15303305</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565440</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.2004.45</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Enhancement of Dopaminergic Differentiation in Proliferating Midbrain Neuroblasts by Sonic Hedgehog and Ascorbic Acid</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Volpicelli</surname><given-names>Floriana</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Consales</surname><given-names>Claudia</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Caiazzo</surname><given-names>Massimiliano</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Colucci-D'Amato</surname><given-names>Luca</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1, 2</sup></xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Perrone-Capano</surname><given-names>Carla</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1, 3</sup></xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>di Porzio</surname><given-names>Umberto</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><sup>1</sup>Institute of Genetics and Biophysics &#x0201c;A. Buzzati Traverso &#x0201d;, Via P. Castellino 111, CNR, Naples 80135, Italy</aff><aff id="I2"><sup>2</sup>Institute of Endocrinology and Experimental Oncology, CNR, Naples, Italy</aff><aff id="I3"><sup>3</sup>University of Catanzaro &#x0201c;Magna Grecia &#x0201d;, Pharmacobiology, Roccelletta di Borgia, CZ, Italy</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*Floriana Volpicelli: <email>volpicel@igb.cnr.it</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2004</year></pub-date><volume>11</volume><issue>1-2</issue><fpage>45</fpage><lpage>57</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2004.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2004</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>We analyzed the molecular mechanismsinvolved in the acquisition and maturation ofdopaminergic (DA) neurons generated in vitrofrom rat ventral mesencephalon (MES) cells in thepresence of mitogens or specific signalingmolecules. The addition of basic fibroblast growthfactor (bFGF) to MES cells in serum-free mediumstimulates the proliferation of neuroblasts butdelays DA differentiation. Recombinant Sonichedgehog (SHH) protein increases up to three foldthe number of tyrosine hydroxylase (TH)-positivecells and their differentiation, an effect abolishedby anti-SHH antibodies. The expanded culturesare rich in nestin-positive neurons, glial cells arerare, all TH<sup>+</sup> neurons are DA, and all DA andGABAergic markers analyzed are expressed.Adding ascorbic acid to bFGF/SHH-treatedcultures resulted in a further five- to seven-foldenhancement of viable DA neurons. Thisexperimental system also provides a powerful toolto generate DA neurons from single embryos. Ourstrategy provides an enriched source of MES DAneurons that are useful for analyzing molecularmechanisms controlling their function and forexperimental regenerative approaches in DAdysfunction.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>